  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n17Z synopsis shows an upper level high across much of the \ncentral and southern plains which has led to the clear skies. At\nthe surface the cold air mass which led to many areas starting \nbelow freezing has modified a bit with the south to southwest \nwinds and diurnal heating. A deepening surface low in the front \nrange will continue to keep the winds breezy through the \nafternoon.\n\nOverall weather pattern going into the next work week features the \nupper level ridge moving eastward through the southern plains as a \nlarge upper level trough moves into the desert southwest.  The upper \nlevel flow combined with south to southwest winds and more sun than \nclouds should lead to a gradual warming trend through the middle of \nnext week.  Winds on Sunday should be breezy as the front range low \nwill deepen with an upper level shortwave in southeast Colorado and \nspeeds will range from 20-30 mph with higher gusts.  I opted to go \nhigher than NBM guidance which was in the lower 60s because of the \nwarmer winds and sunshine along with GFS and NAM hinting at upper \n60s for highs.  Monday should be a bit cooler with more cloud cover \nand the trough moving from the 4 corners region to Nebraska through \nthe day.  A few models are hinting at some light QPF however I opted \nto keep rain chances less than 10% given the lower level moisture \nshould still be limited.\n\nLong range ensembles have a ridge building back into the plains for \nthe middle of next week and this should lead to warmer temperatures \nas 850 temps warm into the 17-18(C) range in the afternoon.  NBM \ntrends along with long term temperature forecast are showing upper \n70s to lower 80s for Wednesday and Thursday.  For the end of the \nwork week into the weekend another stout trough moves in from the \ndesert southwest into the central plains and with the combination \nof Pacific moisture aloft and southeast winds at the surface \nbringing in gulf moisture at the surface this could lead to spotty \nshowers and a few storms from Friday afternoon through Saturday. \nGiven this setup is still several days out the POPs are in the 20-\n30% range but this does look like the best opportunity for rain for \nthis week.&&",
